## 16. Request Flow (General Pattern)

```
Client → Express middleware pipeline (helmet, cors, body parser, rate limit)
       → Route match → [protect] → [authorize] → [Joi validator]
       → Controller (thin) → Service (business logic) → Repository (DB)
       → Response
       Any throw at any stage → asyncHandler → errorHandler → consistent JSON error
```

---

## 17. Response Format

Success: `{ "success": true, "data": ..., ...paginationOrCountFields }`
Error: `{ "success": false, "message": "...", "stack": "..." (dev only) }`

---

## 18. Error Handling

Centralized in `middleware/errorHandler.js`. Maps: Mongoose `CastError` → 400, duplicate key (`code: 11000`) → 409, `ValidationError` → 400, `JsonWebTokenError`/`TokenExpiredError` → 401. Stack traces only included when `NODE_ENV === 'development'`.

---

## 19. Validation

Joi schemas exist per-resource in `validators/`: auth, article, search, bookmark, reading history, comment. Pattern: validate → on failure return 400 immediately, before touching any service/DB logic.

---

## 20. Logging

`morgan('dev')` request logging, development-mode only. Cron jobs and ingestion log their own summaries (created/updated/skipped/errors counts) to console. No structured/persistent logging (e.g. Winston, log files) implemented — console only.

---

## 21. Security Features

Helmet (secure headers), CORS (origin-restricted), express-rate-limit (100 req/15min on `/api`), bcrypt password hashing, JWT auth, `mongoose.set('sanitizeFilter', true)` (NoSQL injection protection at the query level, replacing the Express-5-incompatible `express-mongo-sanitize`), Joi input validation on all write endpoints.

---

## 22. JWT Workflow

Issued on register/login with `{ id, role }` payload, `JWT_EXPIRE`-day lifetime. Verified on every protected request in `protect`; user is re-fetched from DB (not just trusted from the token payload) specifically to catch accounts deactivated after the token was issued.

---

## 23. Search Implementation

`GET /api/articles/search` — combines a MongoDB `$text` index search (title/description/content/author) with structured filters (category, country, language, date range, source — accepting either an ObjectId or a fuzzy name match). Known simplification: results sort by `publishedAt`/`views`, not MongoDB's text relevance score. Every keyword search is logged to `SearchHistory` (fire-and-forget, non-blocking).

---

## 24. Trending Implementation

`GET /api/trending` returns: most-viewed articles (sorted by `views`), most-searched terms (aggregated from `SearchHistory`), latest articles (sorted by `publishedAt`), and popular categories (ranked by total views across their articles, not raw article count).

---

## 25. Visualization APIs

Four endpoints reshape existing aggregation data into Chart.js-native `{ labels, datasets: [{ label, data }] }` format: Pie (articles per category), Bar (top 10 sources by article count), Line (daily article counts, zero-filled for gap days), Area (cumulative running total of the same daily series).

---

## 26. Bookmark System

Per-user saved articles. Enforced uniqueness via a compound `{user, article}` unique index at the database level (not just an application check), specifically to close a race-condition window where rapid double-submission could otherwise create duplicates.

---

## 27. Reading History

Per-(user, article) record, upserted (not rejected) on repeat views — `viewCount` increments, `lastViewedAt` refreshes. Reading time is logged via a separate endpoint (frontend reports elapsed seconds), capped at 3600 seconds per call as a defensive measure against bad/malicious client input.

---

## 28. Comment System

Users can add comments (public read, logged-in write), delete their own (ownership verified by comparing `comment.user` to `req.user._id`, both converted to strings for a valid comparison), and toggle likes via `$addToSet`/`$pull` on a `likes` array of user references — which also allows checking whether the *current* user specifically has liked a comment, not just a raw count. Admins can delete any comment (a deliberate addition beyond the original spec, for moderation).